@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.402 = private unnamed_addr constant [1525 x i8] c"The minimum Python version to target, e.g., when considering automatic\0Acode upgrades, like rewriting type annotations. Ruff will not propose\0Achanges using features that are not available in the given version.\0A\0AFor example, to represent supporting Python >=3.10 or ==3.10\0Aspecify `target-version = \22py310\22`.\0A\0AIf you're already using a `pyproject.toml` file, we recommend\0A`project.requires-python` instead, as it's based on Python packaging\0Astandards, and will be respected by other tools. For example, Ruff\0Atreats the following as identical to `target-version = \22py38\22`:\0A\0A```toml\0A[project]\0Arequires-python = \22>=3.8\22\0A```\0A\0AIf both are specified, `target-version` takes precedence over\0A`requires-python`. See [_Inferring the Python version_](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/configuration/#inferring-the-python-version)\0Afor a complete description of how the `target-version` is determined\0Awhen left unspecified.\0A\0ANote that a stub file can [sometimes make use of a typing feature](https://typing.python.org/en/latest/spec/distributing.html#syntax)\0Abefore it is available at runtime, as long as the stub does not make\0Ause of new *syntax*. For example, a type checker will understand\0A`int | str` in a stub as being a `Union` type annotation, even if the\0Atype checker is run using Python 3.9, despite the fact that the `|`\0Aoperator can only be used to create union types at runtime on Python\0A3.10+. As such, Ruff will often recommend newer features in a stub\0Afile than it would for an equivalent runtime file with the same target\0Aversion.", align 1
