@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.420 = private unnamed_addr constant [1255 x i8] c"The directories to consider when resolving first- vs. third-party\0Aimports.\0A\0AWhen omitted, the `src` directory will typically default to including both:\0A\0A1. The directory containing the nearest `pyproject.toml`, `ruff.toml`, or `.ruff.toml` file (the \22project root\22).\0A2. The `\22src\22` subdirectory of the project root.\0A\0AThese defaults ensure that Ruff supports both flat layouts and `src` layouts out-of-the-box.\0A(If a configuration file is explicitly provided (e.g., via the `--config` command-line\0Aflag), the current working directory will be considered the project root.)\0A\0AAs an example, consider an alternative project structure, like:\0A\0A```text\0Amy_project\0A\E2\94\9C\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 pyproject.toml\0A\E2\94\94\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 lib\0A    \E2\94\94\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 my_package\0A        \E2\94\9C\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 __init__.py\0A        \E2\94\9C\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 foo.py\0A        \E2\94\94\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 bar.py\0A```\0A\0AIn this case, the `./lib` directory should be included in the `src` option\0A(e.g., `src = [\22lib\22]`), such that when resolving imports, `my_package.foo`\0Ais considered first-party.\0A\0AThis field supports globs. For example, if you have a series of Python\0Apackages in a `python_modules` directory, `src = [\22python_modules/*\22]`\0Awould expand to incorporate all packages in that directory. User home\0Adirectory and environment variables will also be expanded.", align 1
