@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1098 = private unnamed_addr constant [605 x i8] c"Exempt classes that list any of the enumerated classes as a base class from needing to be moved into type-checking blocks.\0A\0ACommon examples include Pydantic's `pydantic.BaseModel` and SQLAlchemy's `sqlalchemy.orm.DeclarativeBase`, but can also support user-defined classes that inherit from those base classes. For example, if you define a common `DeclarativeBase` subclass that's used throughout your project (e.g., `class Base(DeclarativeBase) ...` in `base.py`), you can add it to this list (`runtime-evaluated-base-classes = [\22base.Base\22]`) to exempt models from being moved into type-checking blocks.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1099 = private unnamed_addr constant [571 x i8] c"Exempt classes and functions decorated with any of the enumerated decorators from being moved into type-checking blocks.\0A\0ACommon examples include Pydantic's `@pydantic.validate_call` decorator (for functions) and attrs' `@attrs.define` decorator (for classes).\0A\0AThis also supports framework decorators like FastAPI's `fastapi.FastAPI.get` which will work across assignments in the same module.\0A\0AFor example: ```python import fastapi\0A\0Aapp = FastAPI(\22app\22)\0A\0A@app.get(\22/home\22) def home() -> str: ... ```\0A\0AHere `app.get` will correctly be identified as `fastapi.FastAPI.get`.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1100 = private unnamed_addr constant [1216 x i8] c"Whether to add quotes around type annotations, if doing so would allow the corresponding import to be moved into a type-checking block.\0A\0AFor example, in the following, Python requires that `Sequence` be available at runtime, despite the fact that it's only used in a type annotation:\0A\0A```python from collections.abc import Sequence\0A\0Adef func(value: Sequence[int]) -> None: ... ```\0A\0AIn other words, moving `from collections.abc import Sequence` into an `if TYPE_CHECKING:` block above would cause a runtime error, as the type would no longer be available at runtime.\0A\0ABy default, Ruff will respect such runtime semantics and avoid moving the import to prevent such runtime errors.\0A\0ASetting `quote-annotations` to `true` will instruct Ruff to add quotes around the annotation (e.g., `\22Sequence[int]\22`), which in turn enables Ruff to move the import into an `if TYPE_CHECKING:` block, like so:\0A\0A```python from typing import TYPE_CHECKING\0A\0Aif TYPE_CHECKING: from collections.abc import Sequence\0A\0Adef func(value: \22Sequence[int]\22) -> None: ... ```\0A\0ANote that this setting has no effect when `from __future__ import annotations` is present, as `__future__` annotations are always treated equivalently to quoted annotations.", align 1
