@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1269 = private unnamed_addr constant [1135 x i8] c"A list of mappings from section names to modules.\0A\0ABy default, imports are categorized according to their type (e.g., `future`, `third-party`,\0Aand so on). This setting allows you to group modules into custom sections, to augment or\0Aoverride the built-in sections.\0A\0AFor example, to group all testing utilities, you could create a `testing` section:\0A```toml\0Atesting = [\22pytest\22, \22hypothesis\22]\0A```\0A\0AThe values in the list are treated as glob patterns. For example, to match all packages in\0Athe LangChain ecosystem (`langchain-core`, `langchain-openai`, etc.):\0A```toml\0Alangchain = [\22langchain-*\22]\0A```\0A\0ACustom sections should typically be inserted into the [`section-order`](#lint_isort_section-order) list to ensure that\0Athey're displayed as a standalone group and in the intended order, as in:\0A```toml\0Asection-order = [\0A  \22future\22,\0A  \22standard-library\22,\0A  \22third-party\22,\0A  \22first-party\22,\0A  \22local-folder\22,\0A  \22testing\22\0A]\0A```\0A\0AIf a custom section is omitted from [`section-order`](#lint_isort_section-order), imports in that section will be\0Aassigned to the [`default-section`](#lint_isort_default-section) (which defaults to `third-party`).", align 1
