@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1277 = private unnamed_addr constant [854 x i8] c"Force `import from` statements with multiple members and at least one alias (e.g., `import A as B`) to wrap such that every line contains exactly one member. For example, this formatting would be retained, rather than condensing to a single line:\0A\0A```python from .utils import ( test_directory as test_directory, test_id as test_id ) ```\0A\0ANote that this setting is only effective when combined with `combine-as-imports = true`. When [`combine-as-imports`](#lint_isort_combine-as-imports) isn't enabled, every aliased `import from` will be given its own line, in which case, wrapping is not necessary.\0A\0AWhen using the formatter, ensure that [`format.skip-magic-trailing-comma`](#format_skip-magic-trailing-comma) is set to `false` (default) when enabling `force-wrap-aliases` to avoid that the formatter collapses members if they all fit on a single line.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1278 = private unnamed_addr constant [162 x i8] c"Combines as imports on the same line. See isort's [`combine-as-imports`](https://pycqa.github.io/isort/docs/configuration/options.html#combine-as-imports) option.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1279 = private unnamed_addr constant [491 x i8] c"If a comma is placed after the last member in a multi-line import, then the imports will never be folded into one line.\0A\0ASee isort's [`split-on-trailing-comma`](https://pycqa.github.io/isort/docs/configuration/options.html#split-on-trailing-comma) option.\0A\0AWhen using the formatter, ensure that [`format.skip-magic-trailing-comma`](#format_skip-magic-trailing-comma) is set to `false` (default) when enabling `split-on-trailing-comma` to avoid that the formatter removes the trailing commas.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1280 = private unnamed_addr constant [82 x i8] c"Order imports by type, which is determined by case, in addition to alphabetically.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1281 = private unnamed_addr constant [185 x i8] c"Don't sort straight-style imports (like `import sys`) before from-style imports (like `from itertools import groupby`). Instead, sort the imports by module, independent of import style.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1282 = private unnamed_addr constant [292 x i8] c"A list of modules to consider first-party, regardless of whether they can be identified as such via introspection of the local filesystem.\0A\0ASupports glob patterns. For more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1283 = private unnamed_addr constant [292 x i8] c"A list of modules to consider third-party, regardless of whether they can be identified as such via introspection of the local filesystem.\0A\0ASupports glob patterns. For more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1284 = private unnamed_addr constant [280 x i8] c"A list of modules to consider being a local folder. Generally, this is reserved for relative imports (`from . import module`).\0A\0ASupports glob patterns. For more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1285 = private unnamed_addr constant [248 x i8] c"A list of modules to consider standard-library, in addition to those known to Ruff in advance.\0A\0ASupports glob patterns. For more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1286 = private unnamed_addr constant [435 x i8] c"Whether to place \22closer\22 imports (fewer `.` characters, most local) before \22further\22 imports (more `.` characters, least local), or vice versa.\0A\0AThe default (\22furthest-to-closest\22) is equivalent to isort's [`reverse-relative`](https://pycqa.github.io/isort/docs/configuration/options.html#reverse-relative) default (`reverse-relative = false`); setting this to \22closest-to-furthest\22 is equivalent to isort's `reverse-relative = true`.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1287 = private unnamed_addr constant [128 x i8] c"An override list of tokens to always recognize as a Class for [`order-by-type`](#lint_isort_order-by-type) regardless of casing.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1288 = private unnamed_addr constant [131 x i8] c"An override list of tokens to always recognize as a CONSTANT for [`order-by-type`](#lint_isort_order-by-type) regardless of casing.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1289 = private unnamed_addr constant [126 x i8] c"An override list of tokens to always recognize as a var for [`order-by-type`](#lint_isort_order-by-type) regardless of casing.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1290 = private unnamed_addr constant [93 x i8] c"A list of sections that should _not_ be delineated from the previous section via empty lines.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1291 = private unnamed_addr constant [482 x i8] c"The number of blank lines to place after imports. Use `-1` for automatic determination.\0A\0ARuff uses at most one blank line after imports in typing stub files (files with `.pyi` extension) in accordance to the typing style recommendations ([source](https://typing.python.org/en/latest/guides/writing_stubs.html#blank-lines)).\0A\0AWhen using the formatter, only the values `-1`, `1`, and `2` are compatible because it enforces at least one empty and at most two empty lines after imports.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1292 = private unnamed_addr constant [216 x i8] c"The number of lines to place between \22direct\22 and `import from` imports.\0A\0AWhen using the formatter, only the values `0` and `1` are compatible because it preserves up to one empty line after imports in nested blocks.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1293 = private unnamed_addr constant [89 x i8] c"A list of modules to separate into auxiliary block(s) of imports, in the order specified.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1294 = private unnamed_addr constant [340 x i8] c"Put all imports into the same section bucket.\0A\0AFor example, rather than separating standard library and third-party imports, as in: ```python import os import sys\0A\0Aimport numpy import pandas ```\0A\0ASetting `no-sections = true` will instead group all imports into a single section: ```python import numpy import os import pandas import sys ```", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1295 = private unnamed_addr constant [506 x i8] c"Whether to automatically mark imports from within the same package as first-party. For example, when `detect-same-package = true`, then when analyzing files within the `foo` package, any imports from within the `foo` package will be considered first-party.\0A\0AThis heuristic is often unnecessary when `src` is configured to detect all first-party sources; however, if `src` is _not_ configured, this heuristic can be useful to detect first-party imports from _within_ (but not _across_) first-party packages.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1296 = private unnamed_addr constant [432 x i8] c"Whether to place `import from` imports before straight imports when sorting.\0A\0AFor example, by default, imports will be sorted such that straight imports appear before `import from` imports, as in: ```python import os import sys from typing import List ```\0A\0ASetting `from-first = true` will instead sort such that `import from` imports appear before straight imports, as in: ```python from typing import List import os import sys ```", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1297 = private unnamed_addr constant [360 x i8] c"Sort imports by their string length, such that shorter imports appear before longer imports. For example, by default, imports will be sorted alphabetically, as in: ```python import collections import os ```\0A\0ASetting `length-sort = true` will instead sort such that shorter imports appear before longer imports, as in: ```python import os import collections ```", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1298 = private unnamed_addr constant [170 x i8] c"Sort straight imports by their string length. Similar to [`length-sort`](#lint_isort_length-sort), but applies only to straight imports and doesn't affect `from` imports.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1299 = private unnamed_addr constant [1123 x i8] c"A list of mappings from section names to modules.\0A\0ABy default, imports are categorized according to their type (e.g., `future`, `third-party`, and so on). This setting allows you to group modules into custom sections, to augment or override the built-in sections.\0A\0AFor example, to group all testing utilities, you could create a `testing` section: ```toml testing = [\22pytest\22, \22hypothesis\22] ```\0A\0AThe values in the list are treated as glob patterns. For example, to match all packages in the LangChain ecosystem (`langchain-core`, `langchain-openai`, etc.): ```toml langchain = [\22langchain-*\22] ```\0A\0ACustom sections should typically be inserted into the [`section-order`](#lint_isort_section-order) list to ensure that they're displayed as a standalone group and in the intended order, as in: ```toml section-order = [ \22future\22, \22standard-library\22, \22third-party\22, \22first-party\22, \22local-folder\22, \22testing\22 ] ```\0A\0AIf a custom section is omitted from [`section-order`](#lint_isort_section-order), imports in that section will be assigned to the [`default-section`](#lint_isort_default-section) (which defaults to `third-party`).", align 1
