@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1362 = private unnamed_addr constant [1159 x i8] c"Whether to use Google-style, NumPy-style conventions, or the [PEP 257](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)\0Adefaults when analyzing docstring sections.\0A\0AEnabling a convention will disable all rules that are not included in\0Athe specified convention. As such, the intended workflow is to enable a\0Aconvention and then selectively enable or disable any additional rules\0Aon top of it.\0A\0AFor example, to use Google-style conventions but avoid requiring\0Adocumentation for every function parameter:\0A\0A```toml\0A[tool.ruff.lint]\0A# Enable all `pydocstyle` rules, limiting to those that adhere to the\0A# Google convention via `convention = \22google\22`, below.\0Aselect = [\22D\22]\0A\0A# On top of the Google convention, disable `D417`, which requires\0A# documentation for every function parameter.\0Aignore = [\22D417\22]\0A\0A[tool.ruff.lint.pydocstyle]\0Aconvention = \22google\22\0A```\0A\0ATo enable an additional rule that's excluded from the convention,\0Aselect the desired rule via its fully qualified rule code (e.g.,\0A`D400` instead of `D4` or `D40`):\0A\0A```toml\0A[tool.ruff.lint]\0A# Enable D400 on top of the Google convention.\0Aextend-select = [\22D400\22]\0A\0A[tool.ruff.lint.pydocstyle]\0Aconvention = \22google\22\0A```", align 1
