@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1574 = private unnamed_addr constant [807 x i8] c"A list of file patterns to exclude from formatting in addition to the files excluded globally (see [`exclude`](#exclude), and [`extend-exclude`](#extend-exclude)).\0A\0AExclusions are based on globs, and can be either:\0A\0A- Single-path patterns, like `.mypy_cache` (to exclude any directory named `.mypy_cache` in the tree), `foo.py` (to exclude any file named `foo.py`), or `foo_*.py` (to exclude any file matching `foo_*.py` ). - Relative patterns, like `directory/foo.py` (to exclude that specific file) or `directory/*.py` (to exclude any Python files in `directory`). Note that these paths are relative to the project root (e.g., the directory containing your `pyproject.toml`).\0A\0AFor more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1575 = private unnamed_addr constant [580 x i8] c"Whether to use spaces or tabs for indentation.\0A\0A`indent-style = \22space\22` (default):\0A\0A```python def f(): print(\22Hello\22) #  Spaces indent the `print` statement. ```\0A\0A`indent-style = \22tab\22`:\0A\0A```python def f(): print(\22Hello\22) #  A tab `\\t` indents the `print` statement. ```\0A\0APEP 8 recommends using spaces for [indentation](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#indentation). We care about accessibility; if you do not need tabs for accessibility, we do not recommend you use them.\0A\0ASee [`indent-width`](#indent-width) to configure the number of spaces per indentation and the tab width.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1576 = private unnamed_addr constant [1134 x i8] c"Configures the preferred quote character for strings. The recommended options are\0A\0A* `double` (default): Use double quotes `\22` * `single`: Use single quotes `'`\0A\0AIn compliance with [PEP 8](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/) and [PEP 257](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/), Ruff prefers double quotes for triple quoted strings and docstrings even when using `quote-style = \22single\22`.\0A\0ARuff deviates from using the configured quotes if doing so prevents the need for escaping quote characters inside the string:\0A\0A```python a = \22a string without any quotes\22 b = \22It's monday morning\22 ```\0A\0ARuff will change the quotes of the string assigned to `a` to single quotes when using `quote-style = \22single\22`. However, Ruff uses double quotes for the string assigned to `b` because using single quotes would require escaping the `'`, which leads to the less readable code: `'It\\'s monday morning'`.\0A\0AIn addition, Ruff supports the quote style `preserve` for projects that already use a mixture of single and double quotes and can't migrate to the `double` or `single` style. The quote style `preserve` leaves the quotes of all strings unchanged.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1577 = private unnamed_addr constant [645 x i8] c"Ruff uses existing trailing commas as an indication that short lines should be left separate. If this option is set to `true`, the magic trailing comma is ignored.\0A\0AFor example, Ruff leaves the arguments separate even though collapsing the arguments to a single line doesn't exceed the line length if `skip-magic-trailing-comma = false`:\0A\0A```python # The arguments remain on separate lines because of the trailing comma after `b` def test( a, b, ): pass ```\0A\0ASetting `skip-magic-trailing-comma = true` changes the formatting to:\0A\0A```python # The arguments remain on separate lines because of the trailing comma after `b` def test(a, b): pass ```", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1578 = private unnamed_addr constant [518 x i8] c"The character Ruff uses at the end of a line.\0A\0A* `auto`: The newline style is detected automatically on a file per file basis. Files with mixed line endings will be converted to the first detected line ending. Defaults to `\\n` for files that contain no line endings. * `lf`: Line endings will be converted to `\\n`. The default line ending on Unix. * `cr-lf`: Line endings will be converted to `\\r\\n`. The default line ending on Windows. * `native`: Line endings will be converted to `\\n` on Unix and `\\r\\n` on Windows.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1579 = private unnamed_addr constant [1419 x i8] c"Whether to format code snippets in docstrings.\0A\0AWhen this is enabled, Python code examples within docstrings are automatically reformatted.\0A\0AFor example, when this is enabled, the following code:\0A\0A```python def f(x): \22\22\22 Something about `f`. And an example in doctest format:\0A\0A>>> f(  x  )\0A\0AMarkdown is also supported:\0A\0A```py f(  x  ) ```\0A\0AAs are reStructuredText literal blocks::\0A\0Af(  x  )\0A\0AAnd reStructuredText code blocks:\0A\0A.. code-block:: python\0A\0Af(  x  ) \22\22\22 pass ```\0A\0A... will be reformatted (assuming the rest of the options are set to their defaults) as:\0A\0A```python def f(x): \22\22\22 Something about `f`. And an example in doctest format:\0A\0A>>> f(x)\0A\0AMarkdown is also supported:\0A\0A```py f(x) ```\0A\0AAs are reStructuredText literal blocks::\0A\0Af(x)\0A\0AAnd reStructuredText code blocks:\0A\0A.. code-block:: python\0A\0Af(x) \22\22\22 pass ```\0A\0AIf a code snippet in a docstring contains invalid Python code or if the formatter would otherwise write invalid Python code, then the code example is ignored by the formatter and kept as-is.\0A\0ACurrently, doctest, Markdown, reStructuredText literal blocks, and reStructuredText code blocks are all supported and automatically recognized. In the case of unlabeled fenced code blocks in Markdown and reStructuredText literal blocks, the contents are assumed to be Python and reformatted. As with any other format, if the contents aren't valid Python, then the block is left untouched automatically.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.1580 = private unnamed_addr constant [1408 x i8] c"Set the line length used when formatting code snippets in docstrings.\0A\0AThis only has an effect when the `docstring-code-format` setting is enabled.\0A\0AThe default value for this setting is `\22dynamic\22`, which has the effect of ensuring that any reformatted code examples in docstrings adhere to the global line length configuration that is used for the surrounding Python code. The point of this setting is that it takes the indentation of the docstring into account when reformatting code examples.\0A\0AAlternatively, this can be set to a fixed integer, which will result in the same line length limit being applied to all reformatted code examples in docstrings. When set to a fixed integer, the indent of the docstring is not taken into account. That is, this may result in lines in the reformatted code example that exceed the globally configured line length limit.\0A\0AFor example, when this is set to `20` and [`docstring-code-format`](#docstring-code-format) is enabled, then this code:\0A\0A```python def f(x): ''' Something about `f`. And an example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: python\0A\0Afoo, bar, quux = this_is_a_long_line(lion, hippo, lemur, bear) ''' pass ```\0A\0A... will be reformatted (assuming the rest of the options are set to their defaults) as:\0A\0A```python def f(x): \22\22\22 Something about `f`. And an example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: python\0A\0A( foo, bar, quux, ) = this_is_a_long_line( lion, hippo, lemur, bear, ) \22\22\22 pass ```", align 1
